memoryAccess is capability
memoryAccess purpose "Authority to read the command line, the environment, standard input, and a child's output into memory"
memoryAccess grants use machine.memory none

systemCalls is capability
systemCalls purpose "Authority to read the command line and the environment, read standard input, and start, wait for, and stop child processes"
systemCalls grants use system.calls none

# As host.py and the C runtime (D-82): `process.argument 0` is the first word after the program's name on its
# command line, split as CommandLineToArgvW splits it (written here: that function is shell32's); a child process
# gets a command line made as subprocess.list2cmdline makes it, and with runCaptured its three standard streams are
# pipes, written and read a piece at a time between other tasks, so a child that fills one pipe while it is being
# written cannot stall it; a child that is still running when a trap ends the program is stopped (D-51).
# machine.state bytes 768 to 831 belong to this module: 768 the list of the program's arguments (0 until it is
# first asked for), 776 the builder of standard input read but not yet taken, 784 whether standard input has
# ended, 792 a count a system call writes, 800 how many bytes a pipe has to read.
argumentsAt is constant
argumentsAt purpose "Where the list of the program's arguments is kept, in machine.state"
argumentsAt type Int64
argumentsAt value 768

stdinAt is constant
stdinAt purpose "Where the builder of standard input read but not yet taken is kept, in machine.state"
stdinAt type Int64
stdinAt value 776

stdinDoneAt is constant
stdinDoneAt purpose "Where whether standard input has ended is kept, in machine.state"
stdinDoneAt type Int64
stdinDoneAt value 784

countedAt is constant
countedAt purpose "Where a system call writes a count, in machine.state"
countedAt type Int64
countedAt value 792

availableAt is constant
availableAt purpose "Where PeekNamedPipe writes how many bytes a pipe has, in machine.state"
availableAt type Int64
availableAt value 800

